.TH CMEMPOOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER 3 "2026-08-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
CMEMPOOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER \- declare buffer for memory pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

CMEMPOOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER(name, elem_count, elem_size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a uint8_t array of the right size for a memory pool with the
given parameters, which the caller then passes to
cmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer_mp(). The size covers the
stride of each element and one status byte for each element, because
the pool keeps those status bytes in the same buffer, after the
entries.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name        Variable name for the buffer
elem_count  Number of elements that the pool holds
elem_size   Size of each element in bytes
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The buffer goes on the stack or into static storage, depending on where
  you declare it
It is useful on embedded systems, and anywhere you don't want a heap
  allocation
The macro declares the buffer with the alignment that every entry of
  the pool is guaranteed to have, which is 16 bytes, so you can pass
  the buffer straight to cmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer_mp()
  without thinking about alignment
The 16 is a fixed constant and not _Alignof(max_align_t), because the
  macro sets the size of an array in the caller's translation unit
  while the library computes its own layout from the same value. The
  two sides can be built by different compilers, and those can disagree
  about _Alignof(max_align_t). A _Static_assert makes sure that 16 is
  enough for the real requirement of the target
An elem_size smaller than sizeof(uintptr_t) is rounded up so that it
  can hold the free-list pointer. The macro does this rounding before
  it computes the buffer size, the same way
  cmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer_mp() does, so the declared
  buffer always holds exactly elem_count elements when you pass it to
  that constructor with the same elem_size
The macro then rounds the stride of each element up to the same
  alignment, and then to a power of two unless both the library and
  this translation unit were built with -DCMEMPOOL_COMPACT_LAYOUT=1.
  This setting must match the library's, and the build checks it: A
  buffer declared here names an object that the library defines under
  the name of the setting it was built with, so a mismatch shows up as
  an undefined reference at link time (to
  cmempool__built_with_fast_layout or to
  cmempool__built_with_compact_layout) and never as a buffer of the
  wrong size at run time. The two settings differ only for an element
  size that is not already a power of two. With either one the stride
  matches cmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer_mp() exactly, so
  every element of the pool, not only the first one, is aligned for any
  object type
The status bytes, one for each element, sit after the elements in the
  same buffer, so the pool needs no separate allocation of its own
elem_count must be nonzero. A _Static_assert rejects two cases at
  compile time: An elem_count of zero, and an elem_count times the
  rounded and aligned elem_size that would overflow size_t
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
CMEMPOOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER(my_buffer, 100, 64);
cmempool *mp = cmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(
    my_buffer, sizeof(my_buffer), 64, false, false, NULL);
if (!mp)
    return 1;
/* ... cmempool_alloc_entry(mp) / cmempool_free_entry(mp, e) ... */
cmempool_destroy(mp);   /* frees the pool object, not my_buffer */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer_mp (3)
